WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.953 --> 00:00:20.468
A set of double doors opened at the rear of the dining room, and Captain Nemo led me into the next room. It was a library.

2
00:00:20.469 --> 00:00:25.896
Tall, black bookcases lined the walls, and they held a huge number of books.

3
00:00:25.897 --> 00:00:30.698
At the base of the bookcases were comfortable-looking couches covered in brown leather.

4
00:00:30.699 --> 00:00:36.356
In the center of the room stood an immense table littered with pamphlets and old newspapers.

5
00:00:36.357 --> 00:00:41.610
Captain Nemo stretched out on a couch while I continued to admire his library.

6
00:00:41.611 --> 00:00:50.689
"This library belongs in a fine European palace," I said. "I'm absolutely astounded when I consider that it's at the bottom of the sea."

7
00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:57.689
"Where could one find greater solitude or silence than under the sea?" replied the captain.

8
00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.435
"Was your study at the museum as perfectly quiet as this?"

9
00:01:01.436 --> 00:01:06.102
"No, sir. You must have six or seven thousand books here."

10
00:01:06.103 --> 00:01:09.848
"Actually twelve thousand," said Captain Nemo.

11
00:01:09.849 --> 00:01:14.768
"I finished with the world on the day the Nautilus plunged beneath the waves for the first time.

12
00:01:14.769 --> 00:01:21.090
On that day I bought my last books and newspapers, and I like to think that people have written nothing more since then.

13
00:01:21.091 --> 00:01:24.006
Please feel free to make use of this library."

14
00:01:24.007 --> 00:01:27.244
I began to examine the volumes on his shelves.

15
00:01:27.245 --> 00:01:36.332
With books in every language, his collection included works on science, ethics, and literature but nothing on politics or economics.

16
00:01:36.333 --> 00:01:40.475
By far the greatest number of books was about science.

17
00:01:40.476 --> 00:01:46.229
I saw a fairly new astronomy book that I knew had been published in 1864.

18
00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:52.150
From that, I concluded that the captain had been living underwater for at most three years.

19
00:01:52.151 --> 00:01:57.546
"Come, let's continue our tour," said the captain, rising from the couch.

20
00:01:57.547 --> 00:02:04.292
A door opened in the wall opposite where we had entered the room, and I followed Captain Nemo into a spacious room.

21
00:02:04.293 --> 00:02:12.213
The splendid lighting revealed that this room was a large gallery filled with treasures from the natural and artistic worlds.

22
00:02:12.214 --> 00:02:19.459
The walls were adorned with more than thirty paintings by some of the most famous artists of the past and present.

23
00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:25.855
The old masters were represented by Raphael, da Vinci, Rubens, and many others.

24
00:02:25.856 --> 00:02:30.784
In the corners of the room stood some admirable statues in marble and bronze.

25
00:02:30.785 --> 00:02:37.855
"Sir," I said, "without seeking to know who you are, I recognize that you are an artist."

26
00:02:37.856 --> 00:02:46.347
"No, merely a collector," said Captain Nemo. "I used to love to collect these beautiful works created by the hands of man.

27
00:02:46.348 --> 00:02:51.847
I greedily sought them out, and I've been able to bring together some objects of great value.

28
00:02:51.848 --> 00:02:56.934
But now they are my last reminders of a world that is dead to me."

29
00:02:56.935 --> 00:03:03.252
He seemed to go into a daydream and forget that I was there, so I took the opportunity to study the rest of the room.

30
00:03:03.253 --> 00:03:10.744
The next thing I noticed was a large organ upon which were strewn works by Mozart, Beethoven, and other great musicians.

31
00:03:10.745 --> 00:03:19.577
In the center of the room was a fountain with a single stream of water that rose into the air. The base was made from a giant clamshell.

32
00:03:19.578 --> 00:03:27.561
Around this fountain stood elegant glass cases filled with wonders from the sea. You can imagine my interest in those.

33
00:03:27.562 --> 00:03:36.228
One case was filled with coral, another with sponges. Yet another contained starfish, sea urchins, and sea cucumbers.

34
00:03:36.229 --> 00:03:44.124
Anyone interested in shells would have fainted at the sight of cases filled with so many rare and expensive specimens from around the globe.

35
00:03:44.125 --> 00:03:56.958
Another case was devoted solely to pearls: pink pearls, green pearls, yellow, blue, and black pearls, the unusual production of diverse oysters from every ocean.

36
00:03:56.959 --> 00:04:00.379
Some of these pearls were larger than birds' eggs!

37
00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:04.045
It was impossible to estimate the value of this collection.

38
00:04:04.046 --> 00:04:09.283
Captain Nemo must have spent millions to acquire these various natural specimens.

39
00:04:09.284 --> 00:04:14.442
I was wondering where his money came from when he interrupted my thoughts.

40
00:04:14.443 --> 00:04:18.600
"You're examining my shells and pearls, Professor?" he said.

41
00:04:18.601 --> 00:04:26.926
"They must be of interest to a naturalist like yourself, but they have a special significance to me because I collected them all myself."

42
00:04:26.927 --> 00:04:31.156
"No museum in Europe possesses such a collection," I said.

43
00:04:31.157 --> 00:04:36.426
"But if I exhaust all my admiration on it, I won't have any left for the Nautilus itself.

44
00:04:36.427 --> 00:04:41.958
I don't wish to pry into your secrets, but I confess I'd like to know more about the submarine.

45
00:04:41.959 --> 00:04:46.251
I see instruments hanging on these walls that I'm not familiar with."

46
00:04:46.252 --> 00:04:52.402
"You'll find these same instruments in my own cabin, where I'll explain them to you," said the captain.

47
00:04:52.403 --> 00:04:55.315
"But first come look at your own cabin."

48
00:04:55.316 --> 00:05:03.307
He led me out of the gallery, through a passage, and into my cabin, which was really too elegant to be called a cabin.

49
00:05:03.308 --> 00:05:10.894
It was furnished with a bed, table, and several other pieces of furniture. I found myself thanking my host.

50
00:05:10.895 --> 00:05:16.140
"Your room adjoins mine, which opens into the gallery," Captain Nemo explained.

51
00:05:16.141 --> 00:05:21.711
We entered the captain's room, which had a severe, almost monk-like appearance to it.

52
00:05:21.712 --> 00:05:27.719
There was only a small bed and a table—no comforts, only the strictest necessities.

53
00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:30.973
Captain Nemo pointed to a seat and I sat down.

54
00:05:30.974 --> 00:05:39.116
He explained that here and in the gallery, he had all the instruments that he needed to navigate the sub and keep track of the weather.

55
00:05:39.117 --> 00:05:42.870
"But how do you power everything?" I asked.

56
00:05:42.871 --> 00:05:45.862
"Ah," said Captain Nemo.

57
00:05:45.863 --> 00:05:55.926
"There is something that reigns supreme onboard my vessel: this agent lights the submarine, warms it, and is the soul of my mechanical apparatus.

58
00:05:55.927 --> 00:05:58.926
This agent is electricity."

59
00:05:58.927 --> 00:06:01.779
"Electricity!" I cried in surprise.

60
00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:10.918
On land electricity was able to produce only limited amounts of power; it wasn't capable of powering such a fast and complicated vessel as the Nautilus.

61
00:06:10.919 --> 00:06:18.180
"But how are you able to do this? The batteries that you use to generate this marvelous force must be depleted very quickly."

62
00:06:18.181 --> 00:06:24.593
"That's true, but my batteries are powered by sodium and someday I'll show you how we mine that."

63
00:06:24.594 --> 00:06:28.245
"Do the batteries produce fresh air for the sub?" I asked.

64
00:06:28.246 --> 00:06:36.824
"We could do that and we have some reservoirs filled with air, but I prefer to rise to the surface every day to replenish our air supply."

65
00:06:36.825 --> 00:06:43.412
"Aha!" I said, for now I understood the sounds we'd heard onboard the Abraham Lincoln.

66
00:06:43.413 --> 00:06:48.983
The Nautilus really did rise to the surface of the ocean to breathe like a whale.

67
00:06:48.984 --> 00:06:53.563
We continued our tour and the captain showed me the engine room and the kitchen.

68
00:06:53.564 --> 00:06:58.817
The door to the crew's quarters was closed, so I couldn't tell how many men were onboard.

69
00:06:58.818 --> 00:07:04.468
He also showed me a small boat that could be released from the sub for fishing or pleasure.

70
00:07:04.469 --> 00:07:07.031
But I was still filled with questions.

71
00:07:07.032 --> 00:07:16.531
"How does the sub descend to great depths?" I asked. "How does it rise back to the surface? I hope I'm not asking too many questions."

72
00:07:16.532 --> 00:07:23.880
"I don't mind answering," replied Captain Nemo. He stared at me for a moment before adding a grim reminder.

73
00:07:23.881 --> 00:07:30.857
"Because you'll never be able to leave the Nautilus to tell anyone what you've seen."
